## Rails API Setup

This video guides you through creating a Ruby on Rails 7 API from scratch and connecting it to a simple React frontend.

*   **Generate the API**: Use the `--api` flag to create a Rails application without the view layer.
    ```bash
    rails new bookstore --api
    ```
*   **Enable CORS**: Add the `rack-cors` gem to your `Gemfile` to handle Cross-Origin Resource Sharing.
    ```ruby
    gem 'rack-cors'
    ```
*   **Configure CORS**: In `config/initializers/cors.rb`, uncomment the configuration block and specify the origins allowed to make requests to your API.

    > [!TIP]
    > For development, you can whitelist your React server's address (e.g., `http://localhost:3001`). Using `origins '*'` will allow requests from any domain, which is insecure for production environments.

## API Structure & Versioning

Properly structuring your API ensures it's maintainable and scalable.

*   **Scaffold Resources**: Quickly generate models, controllers, and migrations using `rails g scaffold`.
    ```bash
    rails g scaffold Book title:string body:text
    ```
*   **Version with Namespaces**: It's a best practice to version your API. This is done by nesting your resource routes inside versioned namespaces in `config/routes.rb`.
    ```ruby
    # config/routes.rb
    namespace :api do
      namespace :v1 do
        resources :books
      end
    end
    ```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> When you namespace your routes, you must also update your controller's file location (`app/controllers/api/v1/`) and its class definition (`class Api::V1::BooksController < ApplicationController`) to match.

## React Frontend Setup

The frontend is a standard React application that fetches and displays data.

*   **Create the App**: Use `npx create-react-app` to generate the project.
*   **Install Dependencies**: Install `axios` for making HTTP requests to the Rails API.
    ```bash
    npm install axios
    ```

## Connecting React to Rails

The core of the connection involves fetching data on component mount and storing it in state.

*   **State Management**: Use the `useState` hook to create a state variable (e.g., `books`) to hold the data fetched from the API.
*   **Data Fetching**: Use the `useEffect` hook to run the data fetching logic when the component first renders.

> [!NOTE]
> The empty dependency array `[]` in `useEffect` ensures the fetch request is only made once when the component mounts. The cleanup function (`return () => (mounted = false)`) prevents state updates on an unmounted component, avoiding a common React warning.

```javascript
// In App.js
const [books, setBooks] = useState([]);

useEffect(() => {
  let mounted = true;
  axios.get('http://localhost:3000/api/v1/books')
    .then(response => {
      if (mounted) {
        setBooks(response.data);
      }
    });
  return () => (mounted = false);
}, []);
```

*   **Rendering Data**: Pass the `books` state as props to a display component and use the `.map()` function to iterate over the array and render each item.

```javascript
// In Books.js
const Books = ({ books }) => {
  return (
    <>
      {books.map(book => (
        <div key={book.id}>
          <h2>{book.title}</h2>
          <p>{book.body}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </>
  );
};
```
